---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const layers = [
  {
    n: 'Apps',
    owner: 'WiNote (Paper), Settings, Files, Wisky.AI, your browser',
    what: 'Ordinary Android apps, except that several share the system UID and one file-selection flow is pinned by component name.',
    how: 'read',
  },
  {
    n: 'Framework',
    owner: 'system_server, services.jar',
    what: 'Patched AOSP: a vendor hardware bridge, a device-check service, a package-install gate, and a lock-credential redirect.',
    how: 'read + probed',
  },
  {
    n: 'Native',
    owner: 'surfaceflinger, installd, wacom_update',
    what: 'The ink pipeline and its 70 transaction codes live in the compositor; waveform installs run through installd; the pen MCU is flashed at boot.',
    how: 'read (disassembly) + probed',
  },
  {
    n: 'Kernel and HAL',
    owner: 'hwcomposer.mt6771, EPDC driver, input, NVRAM',
    what: 'Panel bias via a character device, pen input as an ordinary input device, calibration records via a HIDL service.',
    how: 'read + probed',
  },
  {
    n: 'Silicon',
    owner: 'MT8788, the panel, a W9013 pen MCU, a PMIC',
    what: 'The panel is chosen by an 8-character batch string; the pen microcontroller has its own flash and firmware.',
    how: 'read + probed',
  },
];

const boot = [
  ['preloader', 'first-stage, loads the bootloader', 'probed'],
  ['lk (little kernel)', 'AVB 1.2 verification, fastboot, slot selection', 'read (disassembly)'],
  ['boot', 'kernel + ramdisk', 'read'],
  ['system / vendor / product', 'dynamic partitions inside super, dm-verity', 'read + probed'],
];
---

<Base title="The device" description="A layer-by-layer map of a Viwoods AiPaper: boot chain, root, system services, the ink pipeline, the panel and the pen." wide={true}>
  <h1>The device</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A Viwoods AiPaper: 10.3&Prime; e-ink, Android 13, firmware 3.15.15, MediaTek MT8788, a Wacom pen
    digitizer. Nothing here is a teardown — it is all read out of the shipped software and confirmed
    on the device.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/diagrams/device-map.svg" width="1120" height="736"
         alt="Layer diagram: apps, system_server, native services, kernel and HAL, silicon, and the persistent stores, with vendor additions marked." />
    <figcaption>
      Accent outlines are places where the vendor replaced or extended stock Android. The dashed box
      is a hook whose entire interface is a JSON file.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The five layers</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Who owns it</th><th>What is interesting there</th><th>How</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {layers.map((l) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{l.n}</strong></td>
          <td>{l.owner}</td>
          <td>{l.what}</td>
          <td class="meta">{l.how}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Boot, and where root comes from</h2>
  <p>
    This is the part that surprised me most. The build is a <code>userdebug</code> product signed
    with release keys, and the setuid <code>su</code> binary is part of the signed system image — so
    root over adb works while the bootloader stays locked, verified boot reports <code>green</code>,
    and <code>dm-verity</code> stays enforcing. No partition is modified to get there.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Role</th><th>How</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {boot.map(([s, r, h]) => (
        <tr><td><code>{s}</code></td><td>{r}</td><td class="meta">{h}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Two details worth knowing if you own one: the unlock confirmation normally wants a physical
    keypress, and this tablet has no volume rocker, so the bootloader also takes a token over
    fastboot; and the "unlock allowed" flag lives in the last four bytes of the <code>frp</code>
    partition.
  </p>

  <h2>The private ink pipeline</h2>
  <p>
    The compositor is not only a compositor. It hosts two extra binder services — one publishes a
    buffer queue for stroke rendering, one consumes it — plus a transaction table of roughly seventy
    codes covering refresh mode, panel bias, waveform install, the ghosting filter, and a debug hook
    that dumps the current grayscale framebuffer to a PNG.
  </p>
  <p>
    A stroke does not go through the window manager. It lands in a dedicated 1920&times;2560 buffer,
    gets converted to grayscale on the GPU and dithered, and is pushed to the panel driver without
    waiting for composition or a presentation fence. While the pen is down, full-screen refreshes
    are deferred; when it lifts, a high-quality refresh reconciles whatever the background apps were
    doing.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a second display pipeline, built for one input device, living inside a binary that is
    otherwise AOSP. It is the most interesting thing in the image.
  </p>

  <h2>Read the reference</h2>
  <p>
    The layer-by-layer detail — including the waveform check, the persistent calibration record, and
    the remote-lock path — is in <a href="/docs/platform/">the platform reference</a>, with the
    evidence label on every row.
  </p>
</Base>
